\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Define the Lucas numbers by $L_0=2$, $L_1=1$ and $L_{n+2}=L_{n+1}+L_n$ for $n\ge0$. A Lucas prime is a prime value $L_n$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Are there infinitely many $n\ge0$ such that $L_n$ is prime? \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Number_theory}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Do infinitely many Lucas numbers take prime values?

